    ("gemini", "2025-12-15", "KV cache explained", [
        "What exactly is the KV cache in transformers and why does it use so much memory?",
        "During autoregressive decoding, each new token attends to all previous tokens. Instead of recomputing keys and values for the whole prefix every step, the model stores them: that store is the KV cache. Its size is 2 x layers x heads x head_dim x sequence length x batch x bytes per value. For long contexts it can exceed the model weights. Techniques that reduce it: grouped-query attention (GQA), KV cache quantization to int8 or fp8, and paging as in vLLM's PagedAttention.",
    ]),
    ("chatgpt", "2026-01-09", "Choosing an embedding model for RAG", [
        "I'm building retrieval-augmented generation over my notes. Which embedding model should I use?",
        "For English notes on a laptop, BAAI/bge-small-en-v1.5 is a strong default: 384 dimensions, fast on CPU, good MTEB retrieval scores. bge-base or e5-base are better if you have a GPU. Use sentence-transformers to compute embeddings, normalize them, and use cosine similarity. For bge, prefix queries with the instruction 'Represent this sentence for searching relevant passages:'.",
        "How should I chunk the notes?",
        "Chunk on structure first (headings, paragraphs) and aim for 200-500 tokens per chunk with a little overlap. For chat transcripts, keep a question together with its answer: the user's wording matches future queries and the answer carries the details. Store metadata like date and source with each chunk; it helps filtering and citations.",
        "And where do I store the vectors?",
        "For under a million chunks, FAISS with a flat inner-product index is exact and fast enough. Beyond that, use an HNSW or IVF index. Chroma or Qdrant are alternatives if you want a server with metadata filtering, but FAISS is the simplest for a local-first tool.",
    ]),
    ("claude", "2026-01-18", "FAISS index types: Flat vs IVF vs HNSW", [
        "Explain the FAISS index types and when to use each.",
        "IndexFlatIP / IndexFlatL2 is brute force: exact results, no training, linear time. IVF (IndexIVFFlat) clusters vectors with k-means into nlist cells and searches only nprobe cells: much faster, approximate, and it needs training. HNSW (IndexHNSWFlat) builds a navigable small-world graph: excellent recall/latency without training but more memory. Product quantization (IVFPQ) compresses vectors for billion-scale search. FAISS also supports the GPU through CUDA, which accelerates both training and search by an order of magnitude.",
        "For 200k chunks from my chat history?",
        "Use IndexFlatIP on normalized embeddings. 200k x 384 floats is about 300 MB and a query takes a few milliseconds on CPU. Approximate indexes only pay off in the millions.",
        "Interesting that HNSW is itself a graph.",
        "Yes, HNSW is a proximity graph over vectors, while a knowledge graph is a graph over concepts. They solve different problems: HNSW finds similar vectors quickly; a knowledge graph captures explicit relationships. GraphRAG-style systems combine both: vector search to find entry points, graph traversal to follow relationships.",
    ]),
    ("chatgpt", "2026-02-02", "BM25 vs embeddings: is hybrid search worth it?", [
        "Do I really need BM25 if I have good embeddings?",
        "For personal knowledge bases, yes. Queries often contain exact names: library names, error messages, repo names. Embeddings blur those; BM25 matches them exactly. Hybrid search runs both and fuses the rankings, typically with reciprocal rank fusion (RRF), which needs no score calibration. On most benchmarks hybrid beats either alone.",
        "How do I evaluate whether my retrieval is good?",
        "Build a small benchmark: 30-50 real questions, each labeled with the documents that answer them. Measure Recall@K (did the right document appear in the top K), MRR (how high it ranked) and nDCG. Run ablations: BM25 only, dense only, hybrid. Write the questions from memory, the way you would actually search, otherwise you test with wording copied from the documents and the numbers look too good.",
        "What about a cross-encoder reranker?",
        "A cross-encoder like bge-reranker-base rescores the top 50 candidates jointly with the query; it usually gives a solid boost to precision at the top. It costs latency, so apply it only to the shortlist.",
    ]),
    ("claude", "2026-02-14", "GraphRAG: when does a knowledge graph help retrieval?", [
        "Does GraphRAG actually help, or is it hype?",
        "It helps for multi-hop questions, where the answer needs facts from several documents connected by an entity. Plain vector search retrieves each document independently, so it misses the second hop. A knowledge graph links entities across documents, and graph traversal such as Personalized PageRank can follow those links: HippoRAG showed large gains on multi-hop QA this way. For single-hop factual lookups, hybrid search is usually enough.",
        "My graph is built from co-occurrence of entities. Problem: some nodes connect to everything.",
        "Those are hub nodes. Raw co-occurrence counts favor frequent entities, so they dominate expansion. Weight edges by normalized pointwise mutual information (NPMI), which rewards pairs that co-occur more than chance, and down-weight seeds by inverse document frequency. Also canonicalize entities so PyTorch, torch and pytorch 2 become one node; otherwise the graph silently fragments.",
        "How do I know if the graph is helping?",
        "Evaluate graph expansion against hybrid search on the same benchmark, with a multi-hop slice. If graph expansion only adds noise, it will lower precision at the top. A good design fuses graph scores with BM25 and dense rankings instead of appending graph results.",
    ]),
    ("gemini", "2026-02-20", "Personalized PageRank in simple terms", [
        "Explain Personalized PageRank like I'm a developer.",
        "Imagine a random walker on a graph. At every step it either follows an edge, with probability d (the damping factor), or teleports back to a seed node. Plain PageRank teleports to any node; Personalized PageRank teleports only to the seeds you choose, for example the entities mentioned in a query. After many steps, the visit frequency of each node measures how relevant it is to the seeds. You compute it by power iteration with a sparse transition matrix, which takes milliseconds on graphs with millions of edges.",
    ]),
